<p>
  Select one photo or a hundred in the grid &mdash; or just open one and
  export it on its own &mdash; then click <strong>Export</strong>. The
  export form has four parts, and the defaults (full-resolution JPEG in an
  <code>Exports</code> folder) need no changes. Click
  <strong>Export</strong> at the bottom when you are ready, and
  LightPhotos bakes your adjustments into brand-new files.
</p>

<h2>The export form</h2>
<ul class="lp-article-list">
  <li>
    <strong>Destination.</strong> Choose <strong>Folder</strong> or
    <a href="/docs/immich.html">Immich</a>. Under
    <strong>Location</strong>, <strong>Exports subfolder</strong> writes
    into an <code>Exports</code> folder inside the folder you opened,
    created the first time you export. In the desktop app,
    <strong>Another folder</strong> sends the files anywhere else.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>File.</strong> <strong>File name</strong> is a template:
    <code>{"{name}"}</code> is the photo's own name and
    <code>{"{seq}"}</code> its number in this export, so
    <code>{"trip-{seq}"}</code> numbers the files in order. The form shows
    an example name as you type.
    <strong>Format</strong> is JPEG, PNG, TIFF (16-bit) or WebP
    (lossless), plus AVIF in the Windows and Linux apps and on recent
    macOS. For JPEG, <strong>File size</strong> caps each file, for
    sites that limit uploads.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Output.</strong> <strong>Size</strong> is
    <strong>Full resolution</strong> or a long edge in pixels.
    <strong>Sharpen For</strong> adds sharpening after the resize, tuned
    for Screen, Matte Paper or Glossy Paper at Low, Standard or High. It
    is off by default and comes on top of the Sharpening slider.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Metadata.</strong> The capture date, camera, lens and exposure
    always go into the file, and captions and comments never do. <strong>Location (GPS)</strong> and
    <strong>Camera serial numbers and owner</strong> are off unless you
    tick them. Type a <strong>Copyright</strong> line to write it into every export;
    leave it empty to keep each photo's own.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>What exporting does and does not do</h2>
<ul class="lp-article-list">
  <li>
    <strong>The originals are left exactly as they were.</strong> Your edits
    stay in place too, so you can change a slider tomorrow and export again
    &mdash; exporting never consumes or overwrites anything.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Exports are ordinary files.</strong> Open them in anything,
    attach them to an email, hand them to a print shop. Nothing about them
    needs LightPhotos to still exist.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Nothing is uploaded.</strong> The decode, the adjustments and
    the encode all happen on your own machine, and only the files you
    selected are written &mdash; the rest of the folder is untouched.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Deleting is different.</strong> <strong>Delete</strong>
    removes the photo itself: the desktop app moves it to the Trash, and
    the browser version deletes it from disk with no undo. Exporting is
    the safe way to make something new.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  That is the whole loop. If you have not read the earlier steps, start at
  <a href="/docs/open-the-app.html">opening the app</a>; the
  <a href="/docs/shortcuts.html">keyboard shortcuts</a> are what make the
  second shoot twice as fast as the first.
</p>
